Now I see a critical issue in `decode_mysql_quoted`:

```c
static int decode_mysql_quoted(struct flb_parser_dec *dec,
                               char *in_buf, size_t in_size,
                               char **out_buf, size_t *out_size, int *out_type)
{
    int len;
    if(in_size < 2) {
        dec->buffer[0] = in_buf[0];
        dec->buffer[1] = 0;
        ...
    }
    else if(in_buf[0] == '\'' && in_buf[in_size-1] == '\'') {
        len = flb_mysql_unquote_string(in_buf+1, in_size-2, &dec->buffer);
        ...
    }
    ...
    else {
        memcpy(dec->buffer, in_buf, in_size);
        dec->buffer[in_size] = 0;
        ...
    }
```

In the `else` branch, `memcpy(dec->buffer, in_buf, in_size)` copies `in_size` bytes to `dec->buffer`. But `dec->buffer` was sized to `FLB_PARSER_DEC_BUF_SIZE` (8KB) initially, and then increased to at least `flb_sds_alloc(data_sds)` in `flb_parser_decoder_do`. Since `in_size == flb_sds_len(data_sds) <= flb_sds_alloc(data_sds)`, this should be safe.

But wait - `flb_mysql_unquote_string` can write MORE bytes than the input! Let me look at it:

```c
int flb_mysql_unquote_string(char *buf, int buf_len, char **unesc_buf)
{
    ...
    while (i < buf_len) {
        if ((n = buf[i++]) != '\\') {
            p[j++] = n;
        } else if(i >= buf_len) {
            p[j++] = n;
        } else {
            n = buf[i++];
            switch(n) {
            ...
            default:
                p[j++] = '\\';
                p[j++] = n;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    p[j] = '\0';
    return j;
}
```

In the `default` case, for input `\X` (2 bytes), it writes `\X` (2 bytes). So output <= input. That's fine.

But wait, `flb_mysql_unquote_string` writes to `dec->buffer`. The buffer was increased to at least `flb_sds_alloc(data_sds)` bytes. And `in_size = flb_sds_len(data_sds)`. So the buffer has at least `in_size` bytes. The output of `flb_mysql_unquote_string` is at most `in_size - 2` bytes (since it processes `in_size - 2` input bytes and each input byte produces at most 1 output byte). So this is safe.

