```c
int flb_unescape_string(const char *buf, int buf_len, char **unesc_buf)
{
    ...
    p = *unesc_buf;
    while (i < buf_len) {
        if (buf[i] == '\\') {
            if (i + 1 < buf_len) {
                n = buf[i + 1];
                if (n == 'n') {
                    p[j++] = '\n';
                    i++;
                }
                ...
                i++;
                continue;
            }
            else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        p[j++] = buf[i++];
    }
    p[j] = '\0';
    return j;
}
```

For input `\n` (2 bytes), it writes 1 byte. For input `\\` (2 bytes), it writes 1 byte. For input `\x` where `x` is not a recognized escape, it writes 1 byte (the `\` is skipped, and `x` is written in the next iteration). So output <= input. Safe.

